1
00:00:00,265 --> 00:00:04,462
A ship leaves the harbor at dawn. Calm water. Engines humming.

2
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:08,686
By sunset, it's gone. No distress call. No wreckage.

3
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:13,532
Not even a single drop of fuel floating on the surface.

4
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:18,778
The entire crew, their gear, their belongings... vanished.

5
00:00:20,265 --> 00:00:22,317
As if they never boarded at all.

6
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:29,068
And this isn't one story. It's a pattern, across every ocean.

7
00:00:30,261 --> 00:00:33,377
Repeating, again and again, for hundreds of years.

8
00:00:35,276 --> 00:00:39,040
Here's the strange part. Most of these ships aren't small, or old.

9
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:44,136
Some are massive vessels, with modern equipment and trained crews.

10
00:00:45,261 --> 00:00:48,792
And they disappeared in calm seas, under clear skies.

11
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:53,099
That's exactly what makes this story so hard to shake.

12
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:58,742
The mind wants a big explanation for a strange situation.

13
00:01:00,263 --> 00:01:04,172
But the truth is often simpler... and harsher, at the same time.

14
00:01:05,266 --> 00:01:09,454
Some ships were found completely empty. No violence. No struggle.

15
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:13,831
As if the crew just stood up, and left everything behind.

16
00:01:15,261 --> 00:01:19,519
Food still on the table. Clothes still hanging. Clocks still ticking.

17
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:23,030
There's no clean ending to close the case with.

18
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:28,634
Investigators fall back on logic. A rogue wave...

19
00:01:30,271 --> 00:01:34,182
a sudden mechanical failure... a human error at the wrong moment.

20
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:38,601
But in some cases, those explanations simply run out.

21
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:44,415
No black box. No witness. Not a single body ever turns up.

22
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:48,872
That's why these stories stay alive. They raise a deeper question.

23
00:01:50,266 --> 00:01:54,820
How does something this size vanish, in a world of satellites and radar?

24
00:01:55,264 --> 00:01:59,478
The ocean is still bigger than every tool we've invented to understand it.

25
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:04,950
The sea doesn't always leave a trace... even when it takes something this large.
